       "prompt": "The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic series is zero. Use $S_n=\\frac{n}{2}(a+\\ell)$ to explain why the first and last terms are opposites.",
       "answer": {
        "short": "$S_n=0$ and $n\\neq0$ force $a+\\ell=0$, so $\\ell=-a$.",
        "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=\\frac n2(a+\\ell)=0\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n$ is positive, $\\frac n2\\neq0$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}a+\\ell&=0\\\\\\ell&=-a\\end{align*}$$\nThe first and last terms are opposites."
